WEBVTT

00:00:00.402 --> 00:00:03.360
So far, every random variable counted something.

00:00:03.902 --> 00:00:05.828
Now X can be any value in an

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:07.333
interval, because we measure it.

00:00:08.196 --> 00:00:10.741
At an athletics carnival, sensors in the starting

00:00:10.762 --> 00:00:13.487
blocks time each sprinter's reaction to the start

00:00:13.508 --> 00:00:13.808
signal.

00:00:14.511 --> 00:00:16.619
One reads zero point three five seconds.

00:00:17.123 --> 00:00:19.853
But a finer timer shows zero point three

00:00:19.914 --> 00:00:22.583
five one seven, with more digits after that.

00:00:23.347 --> 00:00:25.876
So what's the probability that a reaction time

00:00:26.017 --> 00:00:28.807
is exactly zero point three five seconds?

00:00:29.632 --> 00:00:30.136
Make a guess.

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:33.206
Here's an example set of two hundred reaction

00:00:33.247 --> 00:00:34.830
times, from the carnival's heats.

00:00:35.632 --> 00:00:38.618
Time is a continuous random variable, which here

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.302
means it can take any value in an

00:00:40.364 --> 00:00:40.663
interval.

00:00:41.426 --> 00:00:44.571
For an ideal continuous measurement, exact repeats have

00:00:44.672 --> 00:00:47.937
probability zero, so counting each value gives just

00:00:48.198 --> 00:00:48.378
ones.

00:00:49.302 --> 00:00:52.253
Instead, we group the times into classes, each

00:00:52.415 --> 00:00:53.740
a tenth of a second wide.

00:00:54.464 --> 00:00:57.152
Twenty land between zero point one and zero

00:00:57.174 --> 00:01:00.645
point two seconds, then fifty, seventy, forty and

00:01:00.706 --> 00:01:01.026
twenty.

00:01:01.673 --> 00:01:02.384
That's a histogram.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:05.517
Its bars touch, because the classes leave no

00:01:05.579 --> 00:01:05.879
gaps.

00:01:06.561 --> 00:01:08.425
What's the probability that a time is at

00:01:08.465 --> 00:01:10.991
least zero point two, but less than zero

00:01:11.051 --> 00:01:12.013
point four seconds?

00:01:12.695 --> 00:01:15.289
Two classes cover that range, with fifty and

00:01:15.391 --> 00:01:16.134
seventy times.

00:01:16.818 --> 00:01:18.619
So the estimate is one hundred and twenty

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.782
out of two hundred, which is zero point

00:01:20.842 --> 00:01:21.021
six.

00:01:21.864 --> 00:01:24.688
Each class's relative frequency is its count over

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.913
two hundred, and they add to one, because

00:01:27.074 --> 00:01:29.338
every time lands in exactly one class.

00:01:30.069 --> 00:01:32.262
What about a time under zero point two

00:01:32.324 --> 00:01:32.826
five seconds?

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:35.430
That cuts the second class in half,

00:01:35.971 --> 00:01:37.972
and the histogram doesn't say where its fifty

00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:38.752
times sit.

00:01:39.634 --> 00:01:41.904
The usual assumption is that they're spread evenly.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:44.702
Then about twenty five are below zero point

00:01:44.723 --> 00:01:45.245
two five.

00:01:45.951 --> 00:01:47.536
Add the twenty from the first class, and

00:01:47.577 --> 00:01:50.168
that's forty five out of two hundred, zero

00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:51.252
point two two five.

00:01:52.057 --> 00:01:54.004
In an exam, write that assumption down.

00:01:54.648 --> 00:01:56.534
The class from zero point three to zero

00:01:56.575 --> 00:01:59.565
point four has probability about zero point three

00:01:59.646 --> 00:01:59.967
five.

00:02:00.771 --> 00:02:02.480
Shrink it to one hundredth of a second,

00:02:02.762 --> 00:02:04.149
centred on zero point three five.

00:02:04.673 --> 00:02:07.101
With the times spread evenly, it holds a

00:02:07.162 --> 00:02:09.831
tenth as many, so about zero point zero

00:02:09.872 --> 00:02:10.393
three five.

00:02:11.176 --> 00:02:13.421
Shrink it to one thousandth, and it's about

00:02:13.542 --> 00:02:15.747
zero point zero zero three five.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.402
Under that even spread, the probability shrinks in

00:02:19.524 --> 00:02:20.507
step with the width.

00:02:21.392 --> 00:02:24.878
A single exact value has width zero, so

00:02:24.919 --> 00:02:28.045
for a continuous random variable, its probability is

00:02:28.106 --> 00:02:28.465
zero.

00:02:28.975 --> 00:02:32.003
But someone's time really is zero point three

00:02:32.064 --> 00:02:32.886
five something.

00:02:33.469 --> 00:02:36.059
Probability zero isn't the same as impossible.

00:02:36.602 --> 00:02:39.829
Some exact time always happens, but no single

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.995
value carries any probability on its own.

00:02:42.778 --> 00:02:45.443
Intervals do, like zero point three four to

00:02:45.505 --> 00:02:46.907
zero point three six seconds,

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:48.812
and those are the events we'll use.

00:02:49.476 --> 00:02:51.626
The ends of an interval are single values,

00:02:51.788 --> 00:02:53.376
so they carry no probability.

00:02:53.678 --> 00:02:56.266
So including or excluding the ends makes no

00:02:56.308 --> 00:02:56.648
difference.

00:02:57.271 --> 00:02:58.819
That's not true for a discrete variable.

00:02:59.423 --> 00:03:01.948
For a fair die, two to five including

00:03:01.989 --> 00:03:04.474
both ends has probability four sixths,

00:03:04.996 --> 00:03:07.360
but strictly between them it's two sixths.

00:03:07.802 --> 00:03:09.291
The ends carry a sixth each.

00:03:09.921 --> 00:03:11.549
There are infinitely many intervals,

00:03:12.112 --> 00:03:13.901
but one function holds them all.

00:03:14.383 --> 00:03:17.170
The cumulative distribution function, or C D F,

00:03:17.812 --> 00:03:20.379
is F of x equals the probability that

00:03:20.579 --> 00:03:22.585
X is less than or equal to x.

00:03:23.348 --> 00:03:24.957
Cumulative means running total.

00:03:25.681 --> 00:03:28.175
For the reaction times, the data only estimate

00:03:28.396 --> 00:03:28.456
F.

00:03:29.281 --> 00:03:31.423
Add the classes from the left: zero point

00:03:31.565 --> 00:03:34.108
one, zero point three five, zero point seven,

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.112
zero point nine and one.

00:03:37.015 --> 00:03:39.478
Now the probability between zero point two and

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:42.102
zero point four is about F of zero

00:03:42.123 --> 00:03:44.326
point four minus F of zero point two.

00:03:44.887 --> 00:03:47.032
That's zero point seven minus zero point one,

00:03:47.474 --> 00:03:48.155
zero point six,

00:03:48.857 --> 00:03:49.538
the same as before.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:52.582
As x moves right, the event that X

00:03:52.742 --> 00:03:55.505
is at most x can only gain values,

00:03:55.725 --> 00:03:56.346
never lose them.

00:03:56.846 --> 00:03:58.656
So F never decreases.

00:03:59.259 --> 00:04:00.816
We say it's non-decreasing.

00:04:01.399 --> 00:04:03.521
It starts at zero, and for this bounded

00:04:03.581 --> 00:04:05.823
example, it reaches one beyond the range.

00:04:06.744 --> 00:04:09.049
For a continuous variable it has no jumps

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.515
either, because a jump would mean one value

00:04:11.597 --> 00:04:12.498
carried probability.

00:04:13.261 --> 00:04:15.070
Now a C D F from a formula.

00:04:15.752 --> 00:04:17.856
F of x is x squared over nine

00:04:18.056 --> 00:04:19.519
for x from zero to three,

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:22.385
zero below that, and one above.

00:04:23.153 --> 00:04:23.682
Is it valid?

00:04:24.387 --> 00:04:26.713
At zero it's zero, and at three it's

00:04:26.913 --> 00:04:28.858
one, so the pieces join without a jump.

00:04:29.559 --> 00:04:32.050
Its derivative, two x over nine, is never

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.299
negative there, so F never decreases.

00:04:35.164 --> 00:04:36.738
Graph it by hand or with a graphing

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:36.960
app.

00:04:37.505 --> 00:04:38.387
It's flat at zero,

00:04:38.909 --> 00:04:41.896
leaves the origin flat, then curves up more

00:04:41.998 --> 00:04:44.363
and more steeply to the point three, one,

00:04:44.786 --> 00:04:46.088
and stays flat at one.

00:04:46.852 --> 00:04:47.978
It's not a straight line.

00:04:48.608 --> 00:04:50.813
Find the probability that X is more than

00:04:51.015 --> 00:04:52.639
one and at most two.

00:04:53.602 --> 00:04:55.406
That's F of two minus F of one,

00:04:55.949 --> 00:04:58.054
four ninths minus one ninth, which is one

00:04:58.115 --> 00:04:58.375
third.

00:04:59.238 --> 00:05:01.202
The probability that X is more than two

00:05:01.484 --> 00:05:03.689
is one minus four ninths, which is five

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:04.009
ninths.

00:05:04.652 --> 00:05:06.618
And the probability that X equals two is

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.845
zero, because X is continuous.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:10.657
Two short lines to remember.

00:05:11.362 --> 00:05:14.499
First, points carry nothing, and intervals carry the

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:15.062
probability.

00:05:15.826 --> 00:05:18.593
Second, between a and b, take F of

00:05:18.634 --> 00:05:20.900
the bigger end, minus F of the smaller

00:05:20.981 --> 00:05:21.100
end.

00:05:21.803 --> 00:05:23.664
If you ever get a negative answer, you've

00:05:23.685 --> 00:05:24.885
subtracted the wrong way round.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:28.085
Your turn.

00:05:28.716 --> 00:05:31.331
The masses of four hundred tomatoes are grouped

00:05:31.413 --> 00:05:32.136
into classes.

00:05:32.781 --> 00:05:35.228
Estimate the probability that a tomato's mass is

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:37.576
at least two hundred and twenty five grams.

00:05:38.319 --> 00:05:39.161
Pause here to answer.

00:05:52.788 --> 00:05:54.952
Two hundred and twenty five cuts the two

00:05:55.033 --> 00:05:57.077
hundred to two hundred and fifty class in

00:05:57.179 --> 00:05:57.478
half.

00:05:58.381 --> 00:06:00.602
Assume its one hundred and sixty masses are

00:06:00.643 --> 00:06:03.705
spread evenly, so about eighty are above two

00:06:03.746 --> 00:06:04.546
hundred and twenty five.

00:06:05.372 --> 00:06:06.925
Add the forty in the top class.

00:06:07.309 --> 00:06:09.194
That's one hundred and twenty out of four

00:06:09.255 --> 00:06:09.535
hundred,

00:06:10.058 --> 00:06:10.759
zero point three.

00:06:11.727 --> 00:06:13.182
Zero point five is wrong.

00:06:13.464 --> 00:06:16.098
It counts the whole class, including masses below

00:06:16.219 --> 00:06:17.224
two hundred and twenty five.

00:06:18.943 --> 00:06:19.347
Last one.

00:06:19.971 --> 00:06:22.634
A continuous random variable has F of x

00:06:22.794 --> 00:06:25.497
equals x squared plus two x, all over

00:06:25.578 --> 00:06:27.860
eight, for x from zero to two.

00:06:28.901 --> 00:06:30.984
Find the probability that X is at least

00:06:31.104 --> 00:06:33.527
zero point five, and less than one point

00:06:33.567 --> 00:06:33.808
five.

00:06:34.371 --> 00:06:35.217
Pause here to answer.

00:06:45.988 --> 00:06:46.127
It's

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:49.596
F of one point five, minus F of

00:06:49.797 --> 00:06:50.698
zero point five.

00:06:51.401 --> 00:06:54.308
The endpoints carry no probability, so the mixed

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:55.231
signs don't matter.

00:06:56.235 --> 00:06:58.318
F of one point five is twenty one

00:06:58.439 --> 00:06:59.240
over thirty two,

00:06:59.882 --> 00:07:02.245
and F of zero point five is five

00:07:02.387 --> 00:07:03.047
over thirty two.

00:07:03.709 --> 00:07:06.015
So the answer is sixteen over thirty two,

00:07:06.397 --> 00:07:07.078
which is one half.

00:07:08.607 --> 00:07:10.079
Minus one half is wrong.

00:07:10.665 --> 00:07:12.314
It subtracts the wrong way round, and a

00:07:12.476 --> 00:07:13.844
probability can't be negative.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:16.703
Next, a new question.

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:20.261
Here's F of x equals x squared over

00:07:20.322 --> 00:07:20.924
nine again.

00:07:21.566 --> 00:07:24.628
It climbs slowly near zero, and steeply near

00:07:24.749 --> 00:07:25.048
three.

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:28.159
What does that steepness tell us about where

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:29.464
the values of X are?

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.018
Here are the key notes.

00:07:33.585 --> 00:07:35.018
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:35.059 --> 00:07:35.523
to copy them.
